---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import LookupForm from '../components/LookupForm.astro';

const breadcrumbItems = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'IPv6 Guide', url: '/ipv6' },
];

const pageTitle = 'IPv6 Guide: 128-Bit Addressing, Notation & Global Adoption | myipv4v6.com';
const pageDescription = 'Master IPv6: 128-bit address syntax, zero-compression rules, SLAAC auto-configuration, Happy Eyeballs RFC 8305, and dual-stack migration.';
---

<Layout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl="https://myipv4v6.com/ipv6"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full flex flex-col gap-10">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-b border-neutral-200/80 dark:border-neutral-800 pb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-purple-50 text-purple-700 dark:bg-purple-950/70 dark:text-purple-300 w-fit">
        Next-Generation Internet
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">
        IPv6 (Internet Protocol Version 6)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        The modern standard designed to replace IPv4. Featuring 128-bit addressing, simplified packet headers, stateless auto-configuration, and end-to-end connectivity without NAT.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Search Tool -->
    <div class="rounded-3xl bg-neutral-100/80 dark:bg-neutral-900/60 border border-neutral-200/80 dark:border-neutral-800 p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Test an IPv6 Address</h2>
      <LookupForm placeholder="Enter IPv6 address (e.g. 2606:4700:4700::1111 or 2001:4860:4860::8888)" />
    </div>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <article class="flex flex-col gap-8 text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">1. The Scale of IPv6: 340 Undecillion Addresses</h2>
        <p>
          Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6) expands the address width from 32 bits to <strong>128 bits</strong>. This provides an almost unfathomable address space of:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-neutral-900 text-purple-300 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto my-2">
          2^128 = 340,282,366,920,938,463,463,374,607,431,768,211,456 addresses
        </div>
        <p>
          That equates to approximately 665,570,793,348,866,943,898,599 (6.65 × 10<sup>23</sup>) addresses for every single square meter of the Earth's surface!
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">2. IPv6 Address Notation & Compression Rules</h2>
        <p>
          IPv6 addresses are written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits (each group represents 16 bits), separated by colons:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-900 border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 font-mono text-sm overflow-x-auto my-2">
          Full form: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:1428:57ab<br/>
          Compressed: 2001:db8::1428:57ab
        </div>
        <p><strong>Two Standard Compression Rules (RFC 5952):</strong></p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm sm:text-base">
          <li><strong>Omit Leading Zeros:</strong> Within each 16-bit block, leading zeros are omitted. For example, <code>:0042:</code> becomes <code>:42:</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Double Colon (::) Substitution:</strong> A single contiguous run of one or more 16-bit blocks of all zeros can be replaced with <code>::</code>. This can only be applied once in any address to prevent ambiguity.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-950 dark:text-white">3. How Dual-Stack & Happy Eyeballs Work</h2>
        <p>
          Today's internet functions primarily in a <strong>Dual-Stack</strong> state: routers, operating systems, and servers run IPv4 and IPv6 simultaneously side by side.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you connect to a dual-stacked website, your web browser invokes the <strong>Happy Eyeballs algorithm (RFC 8305)</strong>:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm sm:text-base">
          <li>The browser sends DNS queries for both IPv6 (AAAA record) and IPv4 (A record) in parallel.</li>
          <li>It starts establishing an IPv6 connection. If IPv6 does not respond within ~250 milliseconds, it immediately starts an IPv4 connection in parallel.</li>
          <li>Whichever TCP/TLS connection completes the handshake first wins and carries your web traffic.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
